In step no. 1, the welds were cut in the desired geometry by laser, and if needed, the zinc coating was removed to generate crack-free samples. It has been assured in previous investigations that the nugget geometries and electrode indentations were similar for coated and uncoated samples. In the second step, the specimens were welded with a decreasing electrode force (cf. Table  2 ), and the LME cracks were characterized afterwards in step no. 3. This was done with a stereo microscope capable of magnifications of up to 80x. Only cracks visible at a magnification of maximum 20x were considered in this investigation, as smaller cracks were assumed to not have an influence on the mechanical properties of the weld [ 15 ]. The crack location relative to the welding machine and the cathodic and anodic side were noted. If the crack was formed in a near-circular arc, the crack length  l  was measured according to equation no. 2. If not, the crack length was measured linearly. The distance to the visible nugget center is defined as  r  and the crack angle as  α .
